1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:09,500
In this session, I will demo how to generate Testcase using GitHub Copilot.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,500
To use this feature, I first need a Test Workspace Folder.

4
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,000
This folder is for the item for which I need to generate Testcase.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
I will go to Testing Support.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Here, I will have two options.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
The first option is to create a Test Workspace Folder for an item.

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The second option is to create a Test Workspace Folder for a list of other items.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,500
I will demo the first option first.

10
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,000
I will choose One Item here.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Here, I will have three types of information.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The first information is the database path.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
This folder path contains the database loaded from the Stream or Snapshot.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The second information is the workspace path.

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
I will create a blank folder.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
I will use this folder path to store the outputs generated while the tool is running.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The third information is the item name.

18
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
This is the item for which I need to create the Test Workspace Folder.

19
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
After filling in these three fields, I will click Generate Environment.

20
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,000
After this step is complete, the tool will show a notification.

21
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
At the same time, Visual Studio Code will open automatically.

22
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
It will import the Test Workspace Folder for the item.

23
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Here, I will have the source file.

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:10,000
I will also have two files: test.cpp and test.h.

25
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Besides, I can use the Open output feature.

26
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,000
It will open the Test Workspace Folder for the item I need to create.

27
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
For the second option, I will choose List Item.

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
I also have three important pieces of information.

29
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The first is the database path.

30
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:51,000
The second is the workspace path.

31
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
The third is the List Items path.

32
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:03,000
I will have an Excel file containing the item name and item path information.

33
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
This identifies the items for which Test Workspace Folders need to be created.

34
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Then I will take the path of this file.

35
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
I will enter it into the List Items path field.

36
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Then I will click Generate Environment.

37
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,000
After this step is complete, choose Open output.

38
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Then we will see the workspace folder created inside Item list.

39
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:19,000
After creating the Test Workspace Folder for the item, we need to create a testcase.

40
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
We choose Generate Test Case by A.I. Copilot.

41
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Here, I have two fields.

42
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
The first one is the item path.

43
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:36,000
I will go to the Test Workspace Folder for the item that needs test cases generated.

44
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
For example, this item.

45
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
I will copy the path of this file.

46
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:51,000
After that, I will enter the first piece of information.

47
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The next one is the database path.

48
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
This is just like the previous step.

49
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:05,000
I will also enter the folder containing the database.

50
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Then I will click Auto Define Object.

51
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
While the tool is running, we can view detailed information.

52
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Click Show Execution Log to display it.

53
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
A Terminal window will appear.

54
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
It will show detailed information while the tool is running.

55
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:44,860
After Auto Define Object is complete, we will go to the next step.

56
00:09:44,860 --> 00:09:47,360
The next step is Prepare Get SUTs.

57
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:07,360
In the pop-up, we will see that it found three SUT functions.

58
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:11,360
The dropdown is also updated.

59
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:18,360
We can see the three functions listed here.

60
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:23,360
We will select one of the three functions for which we want to generate a test case.

61
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:25,360
Next, we will have two options.

62
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:31,360
The first option is to generate a test case for the selected function.

63
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:38,360
The second option is to generate test cases for all functions in this item.

64
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:46,360
To make the running process easier to observe, we will open Visual Studio Code.

65
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:52,360
Then we will import the Test Workspace Folder for the item.

66
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:02,360
Now we will demonstrate the first option.

67
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:10,360
To generate a test case for one function, we choose Generate one SUT.

68
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:16,360
We can see that the trigger to generate the testcase is sent here.

69
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:23,360
We will see the information in Visual Studio Code.

70
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:28,360
We will go to Output and choose GitHub Copilot Chat.

71
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:33,360
The generation process information will be shown in this Terminal.

72
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:39,360
We will see a notification when test generation is complete.

73
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:44,360
At that point, the generation process is done.

74
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:48,360
Next, we will click Terminal.

75
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:50,360
We will build this test.

76
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:11,360
Here, we have eight Testcases, and all eight Testcases have passed.

77
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:16,360
Next, we will check the code coverage.

78
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:20,360
Then we will proceed to build Cantata for this test.

79
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:23,360
We will click Build Cantata here.

80
00:12:54,360 --> 00:13:01,360
After Build Cantata is complete, we will check the Test Workspace Folder.

81
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:08,360
Inside the Output folder, there is a Cantata folder.

82
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:12,360
We will open Cantata.

83
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:14,360
Click Tools.

84
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:16,360
Then we will go to the C folder.

85
00:13:18,360 --> 00:13:21,360
Choose TCC.

86
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:24,360
Go to Tools.

87
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:26,360
Go to Cantata.

88
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:29,360
Then we will open this tool.

89
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:41,360
We will create a C Project in Cantata.

90
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:59,360
We will name the project after the item name.

91
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:14,360
We will drag the Cantata folder into the new project we just created in Cantata.

92
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:26,360
I will click this .cov file.

93
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:30,360
Then I will open this file.

94
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:39,360
Here, I can see that the code for the first function is green.

95
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:43,360
This means that these lines of code have been covered.

96
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:47,360
These red lines are still not covered.

97
00:14:55,360 --> 00:15:02,360
Then I will demonstrate the second option, Generate Testcase All SUT.

98
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:09,360
Before clicking this Generate button, I will delete the newly created Testcases.

99
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:17,360
This prevents duplication in the next run.

100
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:30,360
Then I will click Generate Testcase All SUT.

101
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:34,360
This will create Testcases for all functions in this item.

102
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:34,360
After generating the testcases for all three functions in this item, I will build Cantata.

103
00:17:34,360 --> 00:17:41,360
In this step, we can click Build Cantata to build in the tool.

104
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:48,360
Or we can build Cantata from Visual Studio Code.

105
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:52,360
I go to Terminal and select Run Task....

106
00:17:52,360 --> 00:17:55,360
Then I select Cantata++ coverage measurement.

107
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:14,860
In the Terminal section, I can see the values.

108
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:18,360
The code coverage has reached 100%.

109
00:18:18,360 --> 00:18:21,360
We will go to Cantata.

110
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:27,360
We will see that the code has 100% coverage.

111
00:18:27,360 --> 00:18:35,360
I will gradually drag the Cantata folder into the project.

112
00:18:36,360 --> 00:18:38,360
This is in the Cantata tool.

113
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:54,860
Here, we can see that the code in all three functions is already covered.

114
00:18:54,860 --> 00:19:00,860
Therefore, their color changes to green.

115
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:12,360
Now we click Cantata and choose View.

116
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:17,360
Then choose Report Viewer to view this report.

117
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:22,360
We can view the test report here.

118
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:27,360
We can also see the coverage here.

119
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:34,360
This is the end of my demonstration.

120
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:37,360
Thank you for watching.
